return array(
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC and an adult client agreed to supportive counseling. Six weeks later, the LPCC proposes a substantially different trauma-focused intervention with new risks and homework demands. What is the BEST response before beginning the new approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Terminate treatment because the client may decline the new intervention.',
		'option_b' => 'Begin because the client already consented to psychotherapy in general.',
		'option_c' => 'Reopen the informed-consent discussion, explain the material change, risks, benefits, reasonable alternatives, and questions, then document the client\'s informed choice.',
		'option_d' => 'Ask the client to sign a new form without discussing the change.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Declining one intervention does not by itself require termination when other appropriate options may remain.\n\nB. Incorrect. General consent does not automatically cover every materially different later intervention.\n\nC. Correct. A material change in the nature or risks of treatment calls for renewed communication and an informed, voluntary decision rather than reliance on the original intake signature.\n\nD. Incorrect. A signature documents a process; it does not replace meaningful disclosure and understanding.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A mature 15-year-old asks an LPCC for outpatient counseling without a parent present. No emergency is present. The adolescent reports credible fear of retaliation if a parent is involved. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Promise the minor that no parent can ever be contacted.',
		'option_b' => 'Assess and document whether the statutory self-consent criteria are met, consult the minor about parent involvement, determine whether involvement would be inappropriate, and explain confidentiality limits before treatment proceeds.',
		'option_c' => 'Begin treatment and postpone all consent and parent-involvement decisions until later.',
		'option_d' => 'Require parental consent because minors can never authorize outpatient counseling.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Absolute secrecy cannot be promised; confidentiality has legal and safety limits.\n\nB. Correct. California law permits qualifying minors age 12 or older to consent to specified outpatient mental-health services when mature enough to participate intelligently, while requiring a documented parent-involvement analysis after consultation with the minor.\n\nC. Incorrect. Authority and confidentiality boundaries should be resolved at or before initiation, not deferred.\n\nD. Incorrect. California law provides a qualifying minor-consent pathway.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An adult client\'s parent pays the session fees and calls the LPCC demanding confirmation of the client\'s diagnosis and attendance. The client has not authorized disclosure. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Confirm attendance but not diagnosis because attendance is administrative.',
		'option_b' => 'Identify the client and any lawful authorization or other legal basis for disclosure before confirming treatment or releasing information.',
		'option_c' => 'Provide a verbal summary because only written records require authorization.',
		'option_d' => 'Release the information because the payer is financially responsible.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Confirming attendance can itself disclose the existence of a confidential treatment relationship.\n\nB. Correct. Payment does not make the payer the client, privilege holder, or automatic recipient of confidential treatment information.\n\nC. Incorrect. Verbal disclosures are still disclosures and require a lawful basis.\n\nD. Incorrect. Financial responsibility does not create treatment-information access rights.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'At the start of a telehealth session, a returning client says, "I\'m at the usual place." What should the LPCC do under the current California telehealth regulation?',
		'option_a' => 'Skip location verification when the client has attended at least three prior sessions.',
		'option_b' => 'Document "usual location" because the permanent address is already in the chart.',
		'option_c' => 'Ask only for the city and state unless the client reports an emergency.',
		'option_d' => 'Verbally obtain and document the client\'s full name and address of the client\'s present location at the beginning of the session.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The each-session requirement does not expire after a number of visits.\n\nB. Incorrect. A prior permanent address does not substitute for the required current-session location information.\n\nC. Incorrect. The regulation calls for the address of the present location, not only city and state.\n\nD. Correct. 16 CCR 1815.5 requires the clinician to obtain and document the client's full name and present-location address at the beginning of each telehealth session.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client signs an authorization allowing the LPCC to send a treatment summary to a primary-care physician. The physician later asks for the entire psychotherapy record. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Refuse all communication because treatment records can never be shared.',
		'option_b' => 'Send the full record but mark it confidential.',
		'option_c' => 'Send the full record because a physician requested it for treatment.',
		'option_d' => 'Disclose only information authorized by the client and otherwise permitted by law, and obtain a new or revised authorization if broader disclosure is appropriate and desired.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Properly authorized and lawful disclosures are permitted.\n\nB. Incorrect. A confidentiality label does not cure disclosure beyond the authorized scope.\n\nC. Incorrect. Recipient status does not automatically enlarge the authorization.\n\nD. Correct. An authorization is limited by its scope; a request from the recipient does not expand the client's authorization.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC treating an adult client receives a subpoena from opposing counsel demanding the complete record. The client instructs the LPCC not to disclose anything. What is the BEST initial legal-ethical approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Ignore the subpoena because psychotherapy records are always privileged.',
		'option_b' => 'Send the entire record because any subpoena eliminates privilege.',
		'option_c' => 'Preserve the treatment role, evaluate the subpoena and psychotherapist-patient privilege through the appropriate legal process, communicate accurately with the client, and disclose only if lawfully authorized or ordered.',
		'option_d' => 'Terminate therapy and then release the record because privilege ends at termination.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Legal process should be addressed rather than ignored, even when privilege may apply.\n\nB. Incorrect. A subpoena does not automatically eliminate privilege or require unrestricted production.\n\nC. Correct. A subpoena is legal process that requires analysis; it is not automatically equivalent to a court order or blanket waiver of privilege.\n\nD. Incorrect. Termination does not automatically extinguish privilege or confidentiality obligations.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A person claiming to be an adult client\'s conservator asks an LPCC for the client\'s treatment records but provides no documentation of authority. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Verify the requester\'s identity and legal authority and determine the applicable access rules before confirming or releasing protected information.',
		'option_b' => 'Confirm the client is in treatment but withhold the notes.',
		'option_c' => 'Release the records because conservators always have unrestricted access.',
		'option_d' => 'Ask the requester to describe the client\'s diagnosis to prove the relationship.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Record access depends on the requester's actual legal authority and the applicable access/confidentiality rules, not merely on a claimed relationship.\n\nB. Incorrect. Confirming treatment is itself a disclosure that requires a lawful basis.\n\nC. Incorrect. Authority and scope must be verified; access can also be subject to specific legal limits.\n\nD. Incorrect. Knowledge of clinical details is not a substitute for legal authority and may invite further disclosure.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Identify authority, consent/confidentiality/privilege, the exact requester or decision-maker, and the required sequence before disclosing or acting.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which set of actions belongs to the California BBS telehealth initiation requirements for an LPCC beginning telehealth with a new client?',
		'option_a' => 'Obtain informed consent, discuss potential risks and limitations, provide the license type and number, and document reasonable efforts to identify relevant resources in the client\'s geographic area.',
		'option_b' => 'Require a separate document titled "Telehealth Consent Form" in every case.',
