	array(
		'question_text' => 'A California LPCC closes a case with an adult client whose therapy ends in 2026. Which statement is most accurate about the statutory minimum record-retention rule?',
		'option_a' => 'The LPCC must retain the client\'s health-service records for at least seven years from the date therapy is terminated, subject to longer applicable legal holds or other controlling requirements.',
		'option_b' => 'Every record must be kept permanently.',
		'option_c' => 'The record can be destroyed after one year if the client receives a copy.',
		'option_d' => 'Records may be destroyed immediately after the final bill is paid.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. BPC 4999.75 establishes a seven-year minimum from termination for adult clients; other legal obligations can require preservation beyond the ordinary minimum.\n\nB. Incorrect. California law sets a minimum period but does not establish a universal permanent-retention rule.\n\nC. Incorrect. Giving a client a copy does not eliminate the professional retention duty.\n\nD. Incorrect. Payment status does not replace the statutory retention minimum.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Separate professional status, lawful setting/scope, representation, documentation, and conduct requirements; consent cannot expand legal authority.",
