	array(
		'question_text' => 'An APCC discovers that several claims were submitted using a diagnosis not supported by the current assessment. A manager suggests changing earlier notes so the claims "match." What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Do not falsify the record; promptly involve the supervisor and appropriate billing/compliance resources, verify the clinical facts, correct records or claims through lawful procedures, address client impact, and document completion.',
		'option_b' => 'Delete the original diagnostic information and replace it without an audit trail.',
		'option_c' => 'Change the notes because the billing error has already occurred.',
		'option_d' => 'Ignore the issue because billing staff submitted the claim.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Accurate representation, supervision, record integrity, billing correction, and client impact must be handled together. Falsifying records would create additional professional misconduct.\n\nB. Incorrect. Concealed alteration undermines record integrity and accountability.\n\nC. Incorrect. A prior error does not justify creating inaccurate clinical documentation.\n\nD. Incorrect. A clinician who learns of a material discrepancy has professional responsibilities to address it.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Separate professional status, lawful setting/scope, representation, documentation, and conduct requirements; consent cannot expand legal authority.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC wants to add an unfamiliar trauma intervention after attending a one-hour overview webinar. A client requests the method immediately. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Use the intervention once and decide afterward whether more training is needed.',
		'option_b' => 'Refuse to learn any new method because competence can never expand after licensure.',
		'option_c' => 'Use the intervention because the client requested it.',
		'option_d' => 'Evaluate the competence required, obtain appropriate training, supervision or consultation, and use another competent approach or refer if adequate competence cannot be developed in time.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Competence should be addressed before exposing the client to an unfamiliar intervention.\n\nB. Incorrect. Competence can develop through appropriate education, training, supervision, and experience.\n\nC. Incorrect. Client preference cannot substitute for counselor competence.\n\nD. Correct. Client request and a brief introduction do not establish competence. The counselor must protect client welfare while building or obtaining the required competence.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC notices increasing fatigue and reduced concentration but has not yet made a serious clinical error. Which response is BEST?',
		'option_a' => 'Continue unchanged until a client complains.',
		'option_b' => 'Tell every client the counselor\'s private health details.',
		'option_c' => 'Permanently leave the profession because any fatigue proves incompetence.',
		'option_d' => 'Assess current functioning, obtain support or consultation, adjust workload or duties as needed, and monitor client impact before harm occurs.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Client protection calls for earlier attention to emerging functional decline.\n\nB. Incorrect. Clients generally do not need private diagnostic details to be protected from functional risk.\n\nC. Incorrect. The response should be proportionate to actual functioning and recovery, not stigmatizing or absolute.\n\nD. Correct. Ethical self-monitoring emphasizes current functioning and proportionate prevention rather than waiting for a complaint or assuming permanent incompetence.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'After leave for burnout, an LPCC reports feeling much better and asks to resume a full crisis caseload. Trial duties still show late notes, missed risk cues, and difficulty making urgent decisions. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Ask clients to decide whether the LPCC is fit for crisis work.',
		'option_b' => 'Continue limited or supported duties and base fuller return on demonstrated restoration of professional functioning and risk-management ability.',
		'option_c' => 'Restore the full caseload because the LPCC reports feeling better.',
		'option_d' => 'Permanently exclude the LPCC from clinical work because burnout occurred once.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Professional fitness and assignment decisions cannot be delegated to clients.\n\nB. Correct. Subjective improvement is encouraging, but actual performance and client safety should govern return-to-practice decisions.\n\nC. Incorrect. Self-report alone does not resolve observable performance concerns.\n\nD. Incorrect. Prior impairment does not establish permanent incapacity.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC notices feeling unusually protective and irritated with a client. Which statement is most accurate?',
		'option_a' => 'The LPCC should disclose every emotional reaction to the client.',
		'option_b' => 'The feelings themselves do not establish misconduct, but the LPCC should recognize and manage them if they could influence judgment, boundaries, or treatment.',
		'option_c' => 'Emotional reactions are irrelevant if the LPCC is licensed.',
		'option_d' => 'The LPCC has already committed a boundary violation by experiencing the feelings.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Disclosure is not automatically helpful and may burden the client.\n\nB. Correct. Internal reactions are not automatically unethical; professional responsibility focuses on self-awareness and preventing those reactions from distorting care.\n\nC. Incorrect. Unexamined reactions can influence clinical judgment, boundaries, and decision-making.\n\nD. Incorrect. Internal emotion is not the same as professional misconduct.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC concludes that a client\'s unfamiliar spiritual experiences prove psychosis without asking about cultural meaning, distress, functional impairment, safety, or alternative explanations. When the client objects, the LPCC becomes defensive. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Retain the diagnosis because changing it would weaken professional authority.',
		'option_b' => 'Refer solely because clinician and client have different spiritual beliefs.',
		'option_c' => 'Ask a spiritual leader to make the psychiatric diagnosis.',
		'option_d' => 'Pause the unsupported conclusion, listen to the client, assess meaning and clinical impact in context, consider alternatives, obtain consultation or education, correct unsupported records when necessary, and repair the treatment relationship.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Professional integrity requires correction when the evidence does not support a conclusion.\n\nB. Incorrect. Identity or belief difference alone does not establish incompetence or require referral.\n\nC. Incorrect. Cultural consultation may add context with permission, but clinical assessment remains the clinician's responsibility.\n\nD. Correct. Cultural humility and competence require individualized assessment rather than pathologizing unfamiliar experiences based on clinician assumptions.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An APCC says, "I meet with an experienced licensed colleague once a month for optional advice, so I do not need a formal supervisor." Which response is BEST?',
		'option_a' => 'Clarify that consultation can supplement practice but does not replace the legally required supervision structure for an APCC.',
		'option_b' => 'Tell the APCC that consultation is prohibited during supervised practice.',
		'option_c' => 'Agree because any licensed colleague can serve as a consultant instead of a supervisor.',
		'option_d' => 'Agree if the colleague has more years of experience than the APCC\'s former supervisor.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Consultation and supervision serve different functions. APCC status requires qualifying supervision and related legal documentation/accountability.\n\nB. Incorrect. Consultation may be useful; it simply cannot replace required supervision.\n\nC. Incorrect. Optional advice does not create the legal supervisory relationship required for an APCC.\n\nD. Incorrect. Experience alone does not substitute for the legal qualifications and relationship requirements of supervision.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC considers using an assessment instrument informally translated by a colleague for a client whose primary language is not English. The LPCC has not reviewed validity evidence or cultural/linguistic limitations. What should occur FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Use it if the colleague says the translation is accurate.',
		'option_b' => 'Evaluate the instrument\'s purpose, evidence, cultural and linguistic fit, and the LPCC\'s competence; select an appropriate measure or refer if those conditions cannot be met.',
		'option_c' => 'Administer it and add a disclaimer after scoring.',
		'option_d' => 'Use it because the client asked for testing.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Informal assurance is not a substitute for evidence about the instrument and its use.\n\nB. Correct. Ethical assessment begins with appropriate instrument selection and competence before administration or interpretation.\n\nC. Incorrect. A later disclaimer does not cure an unsound instrument-selection decision.\n\nD. Incorrect. Client preference does not establish validity, appropriateness, or competence.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC receives credible but incomplete information that a colleague may be practicing while impaired. No immediate client danger is established. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Clarify facts within role, assess client risk and applicable duties, and obtain timely ethical, supervisory, legal, or organizational consultation before selecting the appropriate channel.',
		'option_b' => 'Publicly accuse the colleague so clients can protect themselves.',
		'option_c' => 'Ignore the concern because only a client may raise impairment issues.',
		'option_d' => 'File complaints with every possible agency immediately.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. In a nonurgent situation with incomplete information, a proportionate fact-based response protects clients without jumping to public accusation or indiscriminate reporting.\n\nB. Incorrect. Public accusation can spread unverified information and bypass proper processes.\n\nC. Incorrect. Credible information affecting client welfare may require professional attention.\n\nD. Incorrect. Indiscriminate reporting is not a substitute for identifying the applicable duty and authorized channel.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Focus on competence, integrity, bias, impairment, and proportional safeguards; do not confuse an internal reaction or diagnosis with demonstrated functional impairment.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An adult client understands the LPCC\'s recommendation but chooses a different reasonable treatment option. No emergency or involuntary authority applies. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Implement the preferred intervention without consent because it is clinically beneficial.',
		'option_b' => 'Continue persuading until the client agrees with the LPCC.',
		'option_c' => 'Respect the informed choice, discuss benefits and limitations of reasonable options, and continue or arrange appropriate care consistent with client welfare.',
		'option_d' => 'Terminate immediately because disagreement proves noncompliance.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Beneficence does not erase informed choice in voluntary treatment.\n\nB. Incorrect. Persistent pressure after informed decision-making can undermine voluntariness.\n\nC. Correct. Client welfare includes respect for informed self-determination; beneficence does not ordinarily authorize overriding a competent client's voluntary choice.\n\nD. Incorrect. Disagreement with one recommendation does not automatically require termination.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client wants to reduce anxiety enough to return to work. The LPCC personally believes the client should instead end a romantic relationship and repeatedly redirects sessions toward that goal. What is the BEST corrective response?',
		'option_a' => 'Terminate because counselor and client values differ.',
		'option_b' => 'Hide the counselor\'s agenda while continuing to steer the client toward the preferred choice.',
		'option_c' => 'Continue because counselors may substitute their values whenever they believe the client is making a poor life choice.',
		'option_d' => 'Return to the client-defined treatment goals, examine the counselor\'s values and influence, and revise the plan collaboratively unless safety, law, or another legitimate clinical issue requires a different focus.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Value differences alone do not require termination when competent, fair care can be provided.\n\nB. Incorrect. Covert value imposition is inconsistent with client-centered practice and informed collaboration.\n\nC. Incorrect. Professional influence must not become value imposition.\n\nD. Correct. Counseling should not become a vehicle for imposing the counselor's personal values when the client's lawful, clinically appropriate goals can be pursued.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC determines that treatment is no longer effective and that another provider has specialized expertise the client needs. The client is clinically stable. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Discuss the concern and options with the client, plan an appropriate referral or transfer, address records and communication with authorization, and protect continuity rather than ending abruptly.',
		'option_b' => 'Stop scheduling immediately so the client understands the need to find someone else.',
		'option_c' => 'Continue indefinitely because referral may feel rejecting.',
		'option_d' => 'Send the complete record to a specialist before discussing referral with the client.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Ethical termination focuses on client welfare, communication, transition, and continuity rather than abandonment.\n\nB. Incorrect. Abrupt cessation can create avoidable continuity and abandonment concerns.\n\nC. Incorrect. Continuing ineffective care may also harm the client.\n\nD. Incorrect. Referral does not automatically authorize disclosure of the record.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC in a small rural community is asked to serve on the only local disaster-response committee, where a current client also serves. Declining would leave the committee without any behavioral-health professional. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Ask the client to keep therapy secret from everyone on the committee.',
		'option_b' => 'Assess alternatives, discuss foreseeable role and confidentiality risks when appropriate, establish safeguards, document the reasoning, and monitor whether the overlap affects treatment or professional judgment.',
		'option_c' => 'Accept without analysis because community service is separate from therapy.',
		'option_d' => 'Terminate the client immediately before joining the committee.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Secrecy does not resolve the role conflict and may increase shame or boundary strain.\n\nB. Correct. Some community overlaps may be difficult to avoid. The ethical task is contextual risk analysis and safeguards, not automatic acceptance or automatic termination.\n\nC. Incorrect. The overlap can still affect confidentiality, power, boundaries, and judgment.\n\nD. Incorrect. Termination may harm the client and does not necessarily erase the prior relationship or conflict.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'At the final session, a client offers an LPCC a valuable handmade object and explains that refusing it would carry significant cultural meaning. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Refuse immediately because every client gift is unethical.',
		'option_b' => 'Accept the gift and secretly donate it later.',
		'option_c' => 'Accept automatically because cultural respect always overrides boundary concerns.',
		'option_d' => 'Explore the meaning, value, timing, cultural context, treatment impact, policy, and exploitation/boundary risks, then respond in the way that best protects the client and professional relationship.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A categorical rule can ignore context and may itself harm the relationship.\n\nB. Incorrect. Secretly changing the disposition does not address meaning, ownership, or boundary impact with the client.\n\nC. Incorrect. Cultural meaning matters but does not erase power, value, or exploitation risks.\n\nD. Correct. Gift decisions are contextual. Neither culture nor value alone controls; the counselor should assess meaning, power, policy, and treatment impact.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC providing individual therapy to one partner is asked to add the other partner and convert the work into ongoing conjoint treatment. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Begin conjoint sessions because the original client already consented to psychotherapy.',
		'option_b' => 'Tell the new participant that the original client controls all confidentiality decisions.',
		'option_c' => 'Clarify the proposed treatment purpose, who will be clients, each person\'s role, informed consent, confidentiality and record expectations, competence, and whether the role change is clinically appropriate before converting the treatment.',
		'option_d' => 'End individual therapy immediately and begin conjoint work the same day.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The new participant and changed treatment structure require fresh role and consent analysis.\n\nB. Incorrect. Multi-person treatment requires a clear, lawful policy and cannot be resolved by assumption.\n\nC. Correct. Moving from individual to conjoint treatment can change the client unit, roles, confidentiality, records, expectations, and conflicts. Those issues should be addressed before the change.\n\nD. Incorrect. Abrupt role conversion does not resolve competence, consent, confidentiality, and continuity concerns.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A current client sends an LPCC a friend request on the counselor\'s personal social-media account. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Ignore the request permanently because discussing it would be unethical.',
		'option_b' => 'Assess the boundary, confidentiality, treatment, policy, and client-welfare implications; follow the established policy and discuss the issue clinically when useful.',
		'option_c' => 'Accept automatically to avoid hurting the client\'s feelings.',
		'option_d' => 'Block the client and terminate treatment because any online contact is a prohibited dual relationship.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A clinically appropriate discussion may clarify expectations and prevent misunderstanding.\n\nB. Correct. A thoughtful, policy-consistent response is stronger than automatic acceptance, blocking, or termination.\n\nC. Incorrect. Automatic acceptance can create confidentiality and boundary complications.\n\nD. Incorrect. A friend request does not automatically require termination or establish prohibited conduct.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A long-term client loses insurance and says the current fee is no longer affordable. The LPCC\'s practice has limited reduced-fee capacity. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Discuss the financial change transparently, review available lawful options and resources, avoid coercive pressure, and plan continuity or referral if an affordable arrangement cannot be sustained.',
		'option_b' => 'Terminate immediately at the end of the current session.',
		'option_c' => 'Require the client to borrow money before discussing alternatives.',
		'option_d' => 'Continue indefinitely without discussing payment even if the practice cannot sustain it.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Client welfare and access call for transparent discussion and proportionate continuity planning, not a promise the practice cannot keep or abrupt abandonment.\n\nB. Incorrect. Abrupt termination may create preventable continuity problems.\n\nC. Incorrect. Coercive financial pressure is inconsistent with client welfare and professional boundaries.\n\nD. Incorrect. Avoiding the financial issue can create later disruption and unclear expectations.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'After several months, outcome data and client feedback show that counseling is not improving the target problem and the alliance is worsening. What should the LPCC do NEXT?',
		'option_a' => 'Continue the same plan because changing course would undermine confidence.',
		'option_b' => 'Reassess the formulation, goals, intervention fit, client preferences, barriers, and competence; consult or modify, refer, or transfer as indicated rather than simply continuing unchanged.',
		'option_c' => 'Label the client resistant and document noncompliance without reassessment.',
		'option_d' => 'End treatment immediately without discussing the findings.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Persisting despite poor response can increase harm and ignore feedback.\n\nB. Correct. Beneficence and nonmaleficence require attention to treatment response and willingness to revise a plan that is not working.\n\nC. Incorrect. A client-blaming label can obscure treatment mismatch and clinician factors.\n\nD. Incorrect. Reassessment and collaborative planning usually come before abrupt termination when no emergency requires immediate action.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A competent adult client understands the material information and voluntarily refuses an LPCC\'s recommended intervention. No emergency or involuntary authority applies. What is the appropriate response?',
		'option_a' => 'Ask a family member to override the client\'s decision.',
		'option_b' => 'Respect the informed refusal, discuss reasonable alternatives and foreseeable consequences, and document the decision-making process.',
		'option_c' => 'Begin the intervention anyway because the LPCC believes it is best.',
		'option_d' => 'End all services because refusal of one intervention proves the client is unwilling to participate.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Family preference does not replace the competent adult client's decision-making authority.\n\nB. Correct. In voluntary treatment, a competent client may generally refuse a proposed intervention after meaningful informed discussion.\n\nC. Incorrect. Professional preference does not ordinarily override a competent client's informed refusal.\n\nD. Incorrect. Other appropriate treatment options may remain.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A clinic tells every client with limited English proficiency to bring a family member to interpret or the clinic will refuse service. An LPCC sees that this rule is preventing one client from discussing trauma safely. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Tell the family member to sign a confidentiality form and assume the problem is solved.',
		'option_b' => 'Terminate the client because the clinic lacks a bilingual clinician.',
		'option_c' => 'Follow the rule because applying the same policy to everyone is always fair.',
		'option_d' => 'Address the client\'s immediate communication and confidentiality needs using an appropriate language-access solution and raise the blanket policy for organizational review and correction.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A form does not establish interpreter competence, neutrality, or freedom from coercion.\n\nB. Incorrect. Lack of a bilingual clinician does not automatically justify refusing care without exploring appropriate language-access options.\n\nC. Incorrect. A uniform policy can still create discriminatory or clinically unsafe barriers.\n\nD. Correct. The problem includes both individual access/confidentiality risk and a structural policy barrier. Equal rules are not always equitable when they prevent meaningful care.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC wants to advocate for a client\'s workplace accommodation. The client has not authorized disclosure to the employer. What is the BEST approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Clarify the client\'s goals and preferred advocacy role, obtain appropriate authorization before disclosing protected information, and share only what is necessary for the agreed purpose.',
		'option_b' => 'Contact the employer immediately because advocacy always overrides confidentiality.',
		'option_c' => 'Refuse any advocacy because counselors may never communicate with employers.',
		'option_d' => 'Send the complete record so the employer understands the client\'s history.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Advocacy supports client welfare and self-determination but does not eliminate confidentiality or authorization requirements.\n\nB. Incorrect. Advocacy must still operate within privacy and disclosure rules.\n\nC. Incorrect. Appropriate advocacy may be part of care when authorized and within role.\n\nD. Incorrect. Broad disclosure is unnecessary and risks violating confidentiality and client choice.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Center client welfare, autonomy, access, relationship power, and continuity; avoid both paternalistic overreach and passive under-response.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC discovers that yesterday\'s progress note contains a material factual error. What is the BEST correction method?',
		'option_a' => 'Leave the error because completed notes can never be corrected.',
		'option_b' => 'Delete the original note and replace it without showing a change.',
		'option_c' => 'Ask administrative staff to edit the clinician\'s note under the staff member\'s login.',
		'option_d' => 'Preserve the original entry and add a dated, attributable correction or addendum that states the accurate information according to the record system\'s lawful procedure.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Records can be corrected through a transparent, attributable method.\n\nB. Incorrect. Silent deletion can undermine record integrity and appear deceptive.\n\nC. Incorrect. Corrections should be attributable to the appropriate author and follow the record system's procedure.\n\nD. Correct. Transparent correction preserves record integrity and an audit trail rather than concealing the original entry.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A former client posts a harsh public review. The LPCC believes the review is false and wants to reply with dates of attendance and details of the treatment plan. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Post the diagnosis so readers can judge the review fairly.',
		'option_b' => 'Ask another client to respond publicly on the counselor\'s behalf.',
		'option_c' => 'Use a neutral response, if any, that does not confirm client status or disclose confidential information, and address reputational concerns through appropriate nonconfidential channels.',
		'option_d' => 'Disclose the treatment details because the client spoke first.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. This would compound the confidentiality problem.\n\nB. Incorrect. Recruiting clients into a public dispute creates additional boundary and confidentiality concerns.\n\nC. Correct. A client's public statement does not authorize the counselor to reveal attendance, treatment, diagnosis, or other confidential information in a public rebuttal.\n\nD. Incorrect. A public review does not waive confidentiality for the counselor.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'During a telehealth session with a client reporting escalating suicidal thoughts, the video platform repeatedly fails. What should the LPCC do NEXT?',
		'option_a' => 'Continue reconnecting the video indefinitely before taking any other step.',
		'option_b' => 'End the session and send a routine scheduling message for next week.',
		'option_c' => 'Post the client\'s location in a public technical-support forum to obtain help.',
		'option_d' => 'Use the established backup and emergency procedures, verify the client\'s current location and immediate safety, restore reliable contact or activate local emergency resources as indicated, and document the response.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Technical troubleshooting should not delay necessary safety action.\n\nB. Incorrect. Elevated risk requires active safety assessment and continuity planning.\n\nC. Incorrect. This would create unnecessary confidentiality and security risk.\n\nD. Correct. Elevated risk makes continuity and emergency planning more important, not less. The counselor should move to a safe backup pathway rather than spending the whole crisis troubleshooting technology.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An agency policy instructs APCCs to route all suspected child-abuse concerns to a supervisor and forbids staff from making external reports directly. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Wait until the supervisor gives written permission to report.',
		'option_b' => 'Follow the agency policy because employer rules control staff conduct.',
		'option_c' => 'Follow the individual mandated-reporting duty and use internal notification procedures only to the extent they do not impede or substitute for the required external report; raise the defective policy for correction.',
		'option_d' => 'Ignore the agency policy but never tell anyone it is defective.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Supervisor permission is not required for an individual mandated report.\n\nB. Incorrect. Employer policy cannot authorize violation of a mandatory reporting statute.\n\nC. Correct. Organizational policy cannot override an individual statutory reporting duty, and the systemic policy problem should also be addressed.\n\nD. Incorrect. The immediate duty must be met, and a recurring policy risk should also be addressed through appropriate organizational channels.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC learns that a laptop containing client information may have been accessed by an unauthorized person. The facts are incomplete. What should occur FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Contain the incident, preserve relevant evidence, identify what information and systems may be affected, activate the organization\'s privacy/security response, and then determine notification obligations from the facts.',
		'option_b' => 'Delete system logs to reduce embarrassment.',
		'option_c' => 'Ignore the event because no client has complained.',
		'option_d' => 'Immediately tell every client that a reportable breach definitely occurred.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. A proportionate incident response begins with containment and factual assessment; notification decisions depend on the nature of the information, safeguards, access, and applicable law.\n\nB. Incorrect. Destroying evidence impairs investigation and accountability.\n\nC. Incorrect. Security obligations do not depend on receiving a complaint.\n\nD. Incorrect. Notification may be required, but the facts must first be assessed under the applicable rules.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC\'s intake agreement lists the session fee but does not mention a substantial late-cancellation charge. After the first missed appointment, the LPCC imposes the charge. What is the BEST concern?',
		'option_a' => 'Cancellation fees are always prohibited in counseling.',
		'option_b' => 'Fees have no connection to informed decision-making.',
		'option_c' => 'The fee is automatically valid because it appears on the practice website.',
		'option_d' => 'A significant financial policy that could affect participation was not communicated before it was enforced.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The problem is the lack of advance transparency, not a universal prohibition on such fees.\n\nB. Incorrect. Significant financial terms can materially affect whether and how a client participates in treatment.\n\nC. Incorrect. A website posting does not necessarily establish that the client received and understood the material policy before enforcement.\n\nD. Correct. Transparent fee and practice policies support informed choice and reduce unfair surprise or coercion.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An APCC repeatedly omits direct suicide-risk assessment and documentation despite prior feedback, and several high-risk clients remain assigned. What is the BEST supervisory response?',
		'option_a' => 'Immediately end the APCC\'s career without assessment or authorized process.',
		'option_b' => 'Review affected client risk, take needed protective action, increase observation and supervision, limit or reassign high-risk work as appropriate, set a documented remediation plan with criteria and follow-up, and use gatekeeping if unsafe performance continues.',
		'option_c' => 'Wait until the annual evaluation so the APCC has time to improve independently.',
		'option_d' => 'Provide reassurance only because defensiveness is a normal developmental stage.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Gatekeeping should be evidence-based, proportionate, and follow applicable supervision and organizational procedures.\n\nB. Correct. Effective supervision protects current clients while providing specific remediation, monitoring, and accountability rather than waiting passively or using unsupported punishment.\n\nC. Incorrect. Ongoing high-risk omissions require timely intervention.\n\nD. Incorrect. Support may help, but it does not replace client protection and remediation.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Look for the practice-system response: clear policy, accurate records, lawful information flow, supervision, technology safeguards, and documented follow-through.",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A school counselor asks an LPCC for "everything you have" about a shared adolescent client so the school team can plan accommodations. The client/family authorization permits coordination but is limited to information relevant to school functioning. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Clarify the purpose and recipient, share only information within the authorization and reasonably relevant to the coordination purpose, and document the disclosure.',
